A||N||6||HotFieroGT||Question about auto tranny to engine mating||||1||
Z||000000||HotFieroGT||07-13-2001||11:27 PM||||Hi everybody,<P>Just finished mating my engine and tranny on my 88 GT auto.  Had a little trouble getting them together so I pulled them apart to start again.  Noticed the little sleeve inside the torque converter had some gouges on it from sliding the engine onto the splined trans shaft.  Will this cause a leak or is this sleeve just a guide when mating the two together?  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||07-14-2001||10:31 AM||||Hey GT!<BR>If the scratches are on the inside of the sleeve, no problem...<BR>But if they are on the outside, BIG problem!<BR>Be sure to turn the torque converter when installing it, so it engages the pump...<BR>Else it may cause damage to the pump...<BR>crash...<p>[This message has been edited by Fierowrecker (edited 07-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||HotFieroGT||07-14-2001||12:56 PM||||Hey Fierowrecker,<P>Thanks for the reassurance.  I see on the outside where there is a seal that runs there and I was careful with that but it is almost impossible to keep from scratching that inside sleeve.  I went to a trans shop and got a new one and replaced the last one when I too the engine apart and here I go and gouge the new one!  It is good to know that I don't have to pull them apart again and fix it again!<P>Brooke||||reg||1
Z||000003||DRH||07-14-2001||01:21 PM||||Are you saying you are putting them together with the torque convertor already mounted to the flywheel?<P>If so, don't do that! You are asking for big trouble with the seals, front pump, TC, etc...<P>Before mating them, mount the torque converter in the tranny making sure it is rotated so that it goes all the way in. Bolt the bell housing on. It should mate right up. If the torque convertor and flywheel interfere, you didn't get the TC all the way in. Then from underneath, rotate the TC until a hole lines up on the flywheel and put a bolt in finger tight. Rotate the flywheel and repeat until all are in. Then rotate and repeat to tighten...<P>||||reg||5
Z||000004||HotFieroGT||07-15-2001||01:57 PM||||DRH,<P>Thanks for the info, yes I was doing it the wrong way.  Your method sounds much better.  Would you pull them back apart and install a new sleeve in the torque converter?  When I was putting the engine and tranny together I used a socket on the crank to turn the torque converter and it dropped into place and I put the bolts in to hold the engine and tranny together as they were flush.<P>Thanks for any advice you can give,<P>Brooke||||reg||1
Z||000005||DRH||07-15-2001||04:24 PM||||The biggest risk of putting it together the way you did is damaging the front pump or TC. You should be OK as long as you rotated the crank before you put too much pressure on it.<P>I'm not sure what, if any, damage a gouge on that sleeve will do. I don't think it's a sealing surface, but I'm not sure. Maybe someone else on here can say for sure...<P>Don't feel bad... The reason I figured out what you were doing is because I did exactly that on my 66 Mustang when I was in HS <B>many, many</B> years ago. Only I tightened the bellhousing too much before I turned the TC... That's how I know it can damage the front pump.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||HotFieroGT||07-15-2001||04:35 PM||||DRH,<P>All of my experience with pulling and installing engines has been with manual transmission cars (I really like standard trans).  I also have an 88GT 5-speed, an 1964 Mercury Comet Cylone 289/4speed, and a 1972 Bronco 3speed on the tree.  So this is my first Auto tranny project and it has been a struggle!  The cradle idea for the Fiero is neat until you have to work on it!||||reg||1
